WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.300
Film Courage:&nbsp; Tyler what’s been your experience
looking for an agent or a manager.&nbsp;

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:08.360
I know came from the modeling world in New
York and you had done acting as a child, as

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.360
well.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:16.440
What’s your process like looking for an
agent or a manager?

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:21.210
Tyler Johnson:&nbsp; One thing that a lot of people
I think forget when they’re soliciting representation

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:27.080
is it’s as much an interview for you as
it is for them.&nbsp;

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:33.480
It’s really not a question of who you are
working with or the size of the agency.&nbsp;

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:42.649
I think it has to do with the passion that
your representative has for you and I think

00:00:42.649 --> 00:00:49.490
a lot of times as actors we go into these
meetings looking for a certain outcome and

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:53.373
are all too eager to sign on the dotted line
when we hear the things that we want to hear.&nbsp;

00:00:53.373 --> 00:00:59.399
When in reality, there are a lot of hard questions
that you have to ask in reverse.&nbsp;

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:02.790
This is sometimes hard to do, as an actor
because there is a lot of in security in this

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:03.790
business.&nbsp;

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:09.960
The one thing I have always been reminded
of (and my Mom does so lovingly to make sure

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.119
I stay responsible), but is that the second
you finish your last job, you’re unemployed

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:17.210
and that is the nature of this job.&nbsp;

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:22.810
Sometimes when you’re going for these meetings,
you feel like you’re back on your heels,

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:27.240
as opposed to where you should be, which is
firmly centered.&nbsp;

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:36.060
How can we work together to achieve a mutually
desired result?&nbsp;

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:41.439
So for me, that means I have some questions
when I go in there, too.

00:01:41.439 --> 00:01:49.770
But I’ve been lucky enough, especially recently
to come across a great team, a fantastic team

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:55.939
who I really believe in and we’re new to
one another, so I’mvery excited for the

00:01:55.939 --> 00:01:56.939
future.

00:01:56.939 --> 00:02:02.009
Film Courage:&nbsp;&nbsp; I’ve talked to other actors
and they’ve said that they felt their agent

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:06.150
or manager wasn’t giving them the attention
that they need and these actors understood

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.530
this because they had quote “bigger clients”
that they had to deal with.&nbsp;

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:14.431
However, if an actor is feeling that maybe
phone calls aren’t returned, etc., at what

00:02:14.431 --> 00:02:21.210
point do they say either I’m being too demanding
or that these signs are red flags and I need

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.750
to move on.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:26.250
Tyler Johnson: Great question….that’s
tough to say.&nbsp;

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:30.290
Because I think that there are two sides to
that.&nbsp;

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:37.330
On one side I think actors rely too heavily
on their managers.&nbsp;

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:47.340
They lean on them, their agents and representation
and they can scapegoat them for the success

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:52.510
they are not having at the moment.&nbsp;

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:56.280
Rather than turn it externally, I feel that
a lot of the work should be turned internally

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:01.650
and you have to ask yourself “Have I done
everything that I could possibly do?”

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:09.250
For me, I noticed (to get into specifics)
years and years as an actor I really wasn’t

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:15.380
aware of the materials that were being pitched
on my behalf, things like Actors Access, LA

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:22.310
Casting, etc. – those certain different
‘houses’ and it wasn’t until recently

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:26.070
that I started checking the materials to make
sure they were up-to-date and low and behold

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:31.760
here are all the old relics of all the old
agents I’ve been with, and here is a misattributed

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.290
credit or a missing credits and all these
kinds of things.&nbsp;

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:42.240
For me, before you go blaming others (just
like in life) you’ve got to ask yourself

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.810
“Have I done everything?”&nbsp;

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:51.310
For me that really comes down to not just
90%, 95% or even 99%, but am I doing 100%

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:56.730
of what I can possibly do to help my case
and then at that point I turn the blame to

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.570
the representation and then make decisions
from there.&nbsp;

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:00.570
But fortunately it hasn’t gotten to that.

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:01.570
Question for the Viewers: What do you think
are the most important steps in finding an

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:01.572
acting agent?

